[C2C] STATE FINDINGS · ROUND 17
High — game/src/core/game/GameManagerRealmAdvanceOps.ts — transaction order violates the pinned atomic contract and can grant free Body progress on commit failure. investBodyChapterState(...) mutates player before the substitution plan is validated or any material debit is committed. Only afterward does the code build plan and check allSatisfiable. If that check is false, the function still returns consumed with the Body progression already advanced and no materials removed. Likewise, any later exception while resolving change can leave player progress mutated. Round-10 explicitly pinned validate -> consume -> apply / zero state change on failure. Preflight must happen before the real investBodyChapterState mutation, e.g. dry-run on cloned Body state to determine consumed, fully validate debits/change, commit bags, then apply an infallible progression mutation (or rollback everything on failure).
Medium — GameManagerRealmAdvanceOps.ts + BodyChapterEssenceSubstitution.ts — conversion change can be silently destroyed, contradicting the locked surplus contract. The plan correctly computes covered - consumed as required-grade change, but commit calls materialBag.add(...) and ignores its overflow. The new module even documents that overflow is lost. Round-10 pinned that whole lower-grade surplus is returned; silently dropping it changes the effective exchange ratio depending on bag occupancy and breaks exact-value determinism. The transaction must preflight capacity for the complete change or otherwise preserve all returned units atomically; it cannot intentionally discard overflow.
Medium — game/src/core/simulation/earlygame/EssenceSubstitutionEconomy.ts / PhysiqueEssence.ts — only the Bảo→Phàm ratio is actually simulation-derived; phap: 2 is copied without an independently exercised lock. measureEssenceRatioLock() explicitly rejects every substituteRealm except qi_refining, while production authors both bao: 2 and phap: 2. The comment argues that identical drop rates justify carrying the same ratio downstream, but the mission contract says the deterministic economy simulation locks the final adjacent ratios before production data. Either the spec must explicitly authorize a derived downstream ratio from the first-hop invariant, or the simulation must exercise/derive the Pháp→Bảo hop as well. As written, one production ratio is not locked by the advertised simulation path.
The core planner math is otherwise coherent: substitution is downward-only, nearest-higher-first, ratios are adjacent and integer, multi-hop yield is the adjacent product, required-grade inventory is spent first, and the resolver boundary delegates namespace qualification to bodyChapterEssenceGrade. With integer ratios, the stated per-hop floor is naturally exact at each hop.
[C2C] END
